# Claims

A **claim** is one statement that a card asserts and is answerable for. It is a
single sentence that could be true or false, written so that it can be read
without the card. A card is prose, and prose cannot be counted or compared. Its
claims can.

## Where claims live

Claims are written in the header of the card they belong to, under
`metadata.claims`. They stay with the card for the same reason the relations
do: whoever edits the text sees what the card is on record as saying, and
changes both together.

```yaml
metadata:
  category: concept
  claims:
    - id: 2
      text: "What makes a loop strange is that it crosses levels of description, not that it goes round in a circle; without a crossing there is only a cycle."
      evidence: argued
      credence: 0.75
      atom: requires(strange-loop, level-crossing)
      star: levels-not-cycles
```

| Field | Required | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `id` | yes | A whole number, unique on the card. It is never reused or renumbered, because it is half of the claim's address. |
| `text` | yes | The claim in natural language, one sentence, naming its subject. |
| `evidence` | yes | How the card supports the claim: one of the five grades below. |
| `credence` | yes | The probability the card gives the claim, a number from 0 to 1 written with a decimal point. |
| `atom` | no | The claim in the partial logic, when it has been put there. See [partial logic](partial-logic.hmd). |
| `holds` | no | `false` when the claim denies its atom. Left out when it asserts it. |
| `star` | no | The north star the claim falls under. See [north stars](north-stars.hmd). |

## Which statements are claims

A card yields four to seven claims. They are the statements the card exists to
make, in this order of preference:

1. A distinction the card insists on: two things that look alike and are not.
2. A correction the card records: a position that was held and withdrawn.
3. A condition: what something needs, or what is not enough for it.
4. A definition, when the definition is the card's own and not the textbook's.

A claim is taken from the card's text and from nowhere else. It does not add
what a textbook would say and it does not soften or sharpen what the card says.
A claim belongs to one card only. Two cards may make the same claim, each on
its own authority, and then there are two claims, which may or may not agree.

## Evidence and credence

The evidence grade says what kind of support the claim has. The credence puts
a number on it.

| Grade | The claim is | Starting credence |
|---|---|---|
| `proved` | a theorem with a known proof | 0.99 |
| `established` | standard usage or a standard result in the literature | 0.9 |
| `argued` | a position reached by argument in this project's discussion | 0.75 |
| `proposed` | a term coined here, or a design not yet built or tested | 0.6 |
| `conjecture` | marked by the card itself as open | 0.4 |

The starting credence is a prior fixed by the grade. It is lowered by 0.1 where
the card's own wording hedges the claim. Nothing else has moved the numbers
yet, so today they say how a claim is supported and not how well it has
survived testing. They are meant to be moved, by hand and for a stated reason,
and every score in [cross scores](cross-scores.hmd) follows them when they
move.

A credence of 1 or 0 is never written. A claim held with certainty cannot be
revised by any evidence, and nothing in this vault is in that position.

## Addressing a claim

A claim's address is the name of its card and its `id`, joined by `#`:
`strange-loop#2`. The query [claims](../.hql/queries/claims.hql) returns every
claim of the knowledge base as one list, each with its address.

```hql
from claims import claims_of

cards
| filter(c => c.name == "strange-loop")
| flat_map(c => claims_of(c) | map(k => {ref: fmt("{}#{}", c.name, k.id), credence: k.credence, text: k.text}))
```

The types and functions behind that list are the project module
[claims](../.hql/lib/claims/mod.hql). `claims_of(card)` gives the claims of a
card, and `belief(claim)` the probability a claim gives its atom.

## Which cards carry claims

Concept cards carry claims. Question cards, the index and the lens cards do
not: a question card states what is open, and a lens card describes a design.
